WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.930
The 2020 pandemic has changed

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:04.410
life in the city pretty dramatically.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.320
In the height of citywide lockdowns

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.560
it can be very eerie
to look out at a street

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.225
and see no people and no traffic.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.410
Traffic in urban centers
has been a constant,

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:19.840
but under the threat of
coronavirus, many have been

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.530
experiencing what life
could be like without it.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.050
A little after 6:30 and we're

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.710
looking at the West LA area.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.070
What is different in this picture?

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:30.050
Well how about this,
it's probably about 10%

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.120
of the cars that are usually out here.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.490
Traffic in the United States has been

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.830
a growing problem over the last decade.

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:38.350
Especially in San Francisco
where each commuter

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.610
spends 103 hours waiting
in traffic every year.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:46.610
And the cost of congestion
totaled over $5 billion in 2017.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.830
And while it may seem
like the natural solution

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:52.060
to pave more roads and
increase freeway space,

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.020
the city's actually taking
the opposite approach.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.510
Like many European cities,

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:58.663
it's removing cars from its downtown.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.540
And if it works, it might
just help spread this idea

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.563
of building cities
around people, not cars.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.950
Now since I live in Brooklyn and likely

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.270
am not traveling to San
Francisco anytime soon,

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.550
I'm recruiting the help of
my colleague Laura Bliss.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.200
Not only because she
lives in the Bay Area,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.240
but because she's been reporting

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.546
on car-free streets for years.

00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:21.930
The amount of congestion on the streets

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.270
that's grown in recent
years is really striking.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.500
Texas A&amp;M University has published a study

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.410
which says traffic here in California,

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:33.460
and especially here in
the Bay Area, is bad.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.140
Just since 2010, the number of vehicles

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:40.100
entering the city on a daily
basis has grown by 27%.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.170
This growth has been driven

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:43.490
by cheap gas prices, but also the rise

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.687
of ride-handling apps like Uber and Lyft.

00:01:45.687 --> 00:01:48.210
And in San Francisco,
the tech boom has meant

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.700
more people are coming
to the city every day.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.790
There's also a really
sharp uptick in pedestrian

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:56.650
fatalities that the city
has seen in recent years.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.160
And this is an issue that
all of the United States

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.140
has been experiencing
over the last decade.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.770
So the city has done a number of things

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:06.650
over the years to try to
address its traffic problem.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.350
Most recently, the city is launching

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.430
the Better Market Street Plan.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.440
Well another plan is in
the works to transform

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.360
San Francisco's Market Street.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.210
It sure looks great on paper.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.263
A mega makeover of San
Francisco's Mid-Market Street.

00:02:20.263 --> 00:02:22.280
What you will not be seeing?

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.113
Cars.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.040
All the way back to the
dawn of automobiles,

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.920
Market Street has struggled with a diverse

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.610
amount of transportation needs.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.760
From pedestrians to cyclists,

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.950
cars and public transportation,

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:37.403
and even back in 1906 it
could get a little hairy.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.080
One of the most iconic things about

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.620
Market Street is that it's really

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.290
just kinda almost a free for all.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:48.220
It's about 9 AM.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.430
Just at the end of rush hour.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:52.850
And we're gonna ride all the way

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.273
down to the Ferry Building.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.270
And experience Market Street firsthand.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.140
And all of its problems.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.220
You've got Ubers, you've got private cars,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.540
you've got delivery
trucks, you've got buses.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.556
Pepsi truck just blocking the way.

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:10.690
What is he doing?

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.700
You've also go a historic street car.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.440
That's just kinda throttling along.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:16.730
And even though Market Street

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:18.790
has dedicated bike lanes, when cars

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:20.680
need to turn right off the road,

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.450
those bike lanes kinda
disappear and cyclists

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.723
end up weaving in and out of traffic.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:28.820
Everybody's really happy

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.680
this morning as you can hear.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.480
At best, this can lead
to increased congestion.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.783
And at worst, it could
lead to an accident.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:40.720
The city's Better Market
Street Plan aims to fix this

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.070
by removing private cars
from entering the road,

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:45.540
allowing dedicated lanes for trams, buses,

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.880
and cyclists to run unimpeded
for the two mile stretch.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:50.730
As well as a large renovation project

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.390
to the pedestrian walkway.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.800
And while construction will
take years to complete,

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.880
the first step, removing
cars, is already in effect.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:03:59.713
Three!

00:03:59.713 --> 00:04:00.578
Two!

00:04:00.578 --> 00:04:02.244
One!

00:04:02.244 --> 00:04:04.446
Cutting that ribbon took

00:04:04.446 --> 00:04:05.530
well over a decade of debate,

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:08.700
environmental review, and design planning.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.320
They've been talking about limiting cars

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.030
on Market Street almost
since cars were invented.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.260
Or at least since BART was invented.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:17.420
By November of 2019 the San Francisco

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.210
Transportation Administrators
voted unanimously

00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:22.700
to approve of Better Market Street Plan.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.010
Generally speaking,
these kinds of projects

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:27.309
do not go down without a massive fight.

00:04:27.309 --> 00:04:28.366
Whose streets?

00:04:28.366 --> 00:04:29.470
Our streets!

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:32.010
One of the most explosive
fights in recent years

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:33.270
that's gotten a lot of attention

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:34.930
here in the United States was

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:37.583
the 14th Street Busway
Plan in New York City.

00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:39.361
It's not going to be

00:04:39.361 --> 00:04:41.090
your mother's 14th Street anymore.

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:43.200
From 3rd to 9th Avenues it will morph

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.160
into something city officials call a TTP.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.822
It means private cars can
pretty much forget about it.

00:04:48.822 --> 00:04:50.510
Similar to the Market Street Project,

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.690
the 14th Street Busway
was a plan to remove

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:54.410
private cars from entering the road.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.570
The intention here was
to improve bus service,

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:58.720
but some New Yorkers didn't like it.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.580
That's probably gonna just
make the traffic a lot worse.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.150
That don't seem good.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:05.320
It's a bad thing.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:06.570
I am furious.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:07.600
We are furious.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.110
And generally speaking, people oppose

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:14.140
pedestrian zones or car
bans for three main reasons.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.210
First, people fear that
the traffic is gonna

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:18.390
get pushed to parallel
roads and that those

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.930
side streets are just
gonna be stuck in gridlock.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.750
Second, there's the fear of
the loss of on-street parking.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:26.500
And finally, business
owners fear that that loss

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.250
of parking could mean the reduction

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:30.559
of customers and revenue
to their business.

00:05:30.559 --> 00:05:32.810
A plan to ban cars on one of the busiest

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:35.410
cross-streets in Manhattan
hits a roadblock.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:36.640
Arthur Schwartz represents

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:37.933
the group that's suing the city.

00:05:37.933 --> 00:05:41.550
There's a thousand cars an
hour on the side streets.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.370
That is not a good trade-off.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:44.360
For anybody.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.380
If anybody in their right mind thinks

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.250
that there's not gonna be a major impact

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:50.140
because of this, they're crazy.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:52.020
But once the car ban went into effect,

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.050
the data came back and was pretty clear.

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:55.787
There was no Side Street.

00:05:55.787 --> 00:05:57.087
Trafficopalyse.

00:05:57.087 --> 00:05:58.504
Traffic-opalypse.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:02.110
People love to get biblical with traffic.

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:04.260
Carmageddon and...

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.280
This is Trevor Reed,

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:07.280
a transportation analyst at INRIX.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:09.910
And yes, he's also working from home.

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:10.743
Do you play football?

00:06:10.743 --> 00:06:12.937
Does someone else play
football in the family?

00:06:12.937 --> 00:06:15.430
Well I'm back at home with my parents.

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:16.263
Nice.

00:06:16.263 --> 00:06:17.096
I did play football and that's--

00:06:17.096 --> 00:06:18.270
Is this all your childhood stuff?

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:19.310
Yup, yup.

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:20.143
Pretty much.

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:22.970
INRIX worked with a third party

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:24.940
consulting firm, Sam Schwartz Consulting,

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:26.640
to collect data around how the traffic

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.810
changed after the 14th Street Busway.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:31.240
The end result of the 14th Street Busway

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:33.200
was an absolute success.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.150
On all the parallel streets you're seeing

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:37.487
a travel increase in travel times by about

00:06:37.487 --> 00:06:40.230
one to two minutes, which is negligible,

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:44.166
but you're seeing a 36%
improvement in bus speeds,

00:06:44.166 --> 00:06:47.378
which was 5.3 minutes faster on average,

00:06:47.378 --> 00:06:50.508
which corresponded to a 24% increase

00:06:50.508 --> 00:06:54.760
in bus riders, so over
6,000 increased riders.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:56.220
And you saw a spike in bicycle

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:59.830
volumes of between 26 and 50%.

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:01.620
But Market Street is a little different.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.260
Unlike 14th Street that's laid out

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:05.520
in a fairly traditional grid with streets

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.730
running parallel, Market Street only has

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:09.490
one side of parallel street options,

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:11.440
Mission that runs north and south,

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:13.480
and Howard that only runs southbound.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:15.270
And while it may feel
like all that displaced

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:17.370
traffic for Market is
gonna have a bigger effect

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:20.960
on streets like Mission, the
results were almost the same.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.190
In the morning, on
Mission Street Northbound,

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:27.010
we were seeing a change in
the range of 30 seconds,

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:30.840
and then in the afternoon an
increase of about a minute.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.200
In the AM on Mission Street Southbound

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.550
it was a negligible change.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.659
You're talking about 10 seconds
or so at the 9 AM period

00:07:38.659 --> 00:07:42.893
increasing to about a 50
second increase in travel times

00:07:42.893 --> 00:07:46.980
at 10 in the morning, and
your afternoon period change

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:51.390
was again about 10 to 15
seconds during the peak period.

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:53.320
And then overall, Howard Street was really

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:56.860
showing no statistical change.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:58.250
And while there is a small uptick

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:00.130
in traffic delay, Trevor points out

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:01.830
that that's not looking
at the whole picture.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:04.691
It's not just displacement of vehicles

00:08:04.691 --> 00:08:07.010
when you put in these busways, they also

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:11.030
absorb a lot of those
vehicles as new riders.

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:12.230
It's not fate that you're gonna have

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:15.300
10,000 cars go into a point every day.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.160
It changes and people adapt to change.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:22.160
It only took one day for
bike ridership to jump 20%.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:26.360
After a month, that number jumped to 25%.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:30.060
Bus speeds are running
6% faster on average

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:32.530
up and down Market Street, and some lines

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:35.460
have actually seen a 12% improvement.

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:37.610
You know, when you're
measuring your benefit

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:41.280
in thousands of people,
and the negative impact

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:45.361
in a dozen cars, it's
really, it's a no brainer

00:08:45.361 --> 00:08:50.361
as far as the benefit versus
costs on these busways.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.260
And as for how loss of parking

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:53.860
is gonna affect local business?

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:57.110
Vendors think that a
much higher proportion

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:00.090
of their customers are coming
via vehicle than they are.

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:03.860
It's 240 square feet to
park a personal vehicle

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:08.020
and the odds are that's one customer.

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:11.720
No restaurant or store
can survive in an urban

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:16.270
context just based upon people driving,

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:18.620
parking, and going to use that service.

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:19.550
There's actually been proven

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:21.470
a positive economic correlation.

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:23.840
A lot of these, in a lot of the context,

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.860
following the improvement of
bus or bike infrastructure

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:29.200
because you're actually
getting more people

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:31.170
into an area than you were previously

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:32.930
with the on-street parking.

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:34.440
Because of the shelter in place order,

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.170
it's been difficult to create any concrete

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:38.550
economic data around the
Market Street project.

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:40.100
But around the world, evidence has been

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:41.930
pretty strikingly positive.

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:43.000
It used to look like this.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.720
A parking lot with 75 cars parked here.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:49.410
So the city council proposed to take away

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:52.970
60, so 15 left, and
then the merchants here

00:09:52.970 --> 00:09:55.620
said no, we want them all gone.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.560
Because we're right off
the pedestrianized zone

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:00.620
where sales are better.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:03.430
After Central Madrid went car-free,

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:06.430
with a pretty large swath
of its downtown area

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:09.730
now no access to cars, it's seen

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:13.020
nearly a 10% boost to retail sales

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:15.550
for businesses in that pedestrian zone.

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:19.500
And it's also seen
greenhouse gases drop by 32%.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:21.170
But not all car-free street projects

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:22.650
are looked at as successes.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:25.190
In the US during the 60s and 70s cities

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:27.760
created what were known
as pedestrian malls.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:29.260
Streets were congested.

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:30.640
Parking was inadequate.

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:32.760
And pedestrians were endangered.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:34.300
The solution?

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:37.610
Separate cars and pedestrians
for the benefit of both.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.892
In short, what was an
ordinary typical main street

00:10:40.892 --> 00:10:42.900
is now an extraordinary place

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:45.170
designed for the enjoyment of people.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:46.280
It was an attempt to help failing

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:48.480
downtown areas attract
customers as shopping

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:49.850
had moved to more car friendly

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:51.720
shopping centers in the suburbs.

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:53.130
And while some of these malls are still

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:56.640
thriving today, failures
in cities like Chicago

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:59.003
have left a lasting
distaste for such projects.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:02.690
Pedestrian zones, car-free schemes

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:04.900
are definitely not silver bullet solutions

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:06.560
that are just gonna magically fix

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:09.220
an entire city's traffic
problems overnight.

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:10.680
Even the most successful ones

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:13.130
still come with their considerations.

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:16.951
A big one is that people who rely on cars,

00:11:16.951 --> 00:11:19.670
because they have
disabilities, sometimes say

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:22.320
they feel left out of
the planning processes

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:25.050
around making whole corridors car-free.

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:26.510
'Cause you still need
to ensure that they're

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:28.940
as easy to use, accessible, pick up

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:31.100
and drop off zones for those folks.

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:32.540
Especially when public transit

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:34.690
is not incredibly easy to use.

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:36.690
Another issue is that community leaders

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.188
sometimes point out that car-free zones

00:11:39.188 --> 00:11:44.188
are usually created in
thriving commercial corridors,

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:45.740
but some planners say that that could

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:49.650
exacerbate existing socioeconomic gaps.

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:52.460
Especially since low
income people of color

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:54.870
in the United States
are disproportionately

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:58.320
victimized by car crashes,
and very rarely do you

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:01.430
find these car-free zones
in their communities.

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:03.790
It's one of many structural inequalities

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:06.710
built into cities that
marginalize communities

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:09.410
and their allies say must be overcome.

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:10.810
Everyone's learning from each other

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:12.663
and you see what success looks like.

00:12:15.229 --> 00:12:16.110
And it helps that a lot of these

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:18.720
interventions are very affordable.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:19.910
Paint is cheap.

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:22.240
You created a subway
line worth of capacity

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:24.740
on 14th Street with some paint

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:26.260
and a little bit of enforcement

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:30.793
and that cost benefit
is just unbelievable.

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:33.443
And since traffic isn't as large a concern

00:12:33.443 --> 00:12:36.060
for cities during the
coronavirus pandemic,

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:39.180
some are actually closing
off roads to cars entirely.

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:40.640
There are a number of cities that are

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:42.810
intentionally limited or blocking access

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:45.240
to vehicles on neighborhood
streets as a way

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:47.040
to boost access to outdoor space

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:48.680
and create safer corridors for

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:50.730
essential workers by fare or bike.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:52.770
Oakland, California
was one of the earliest

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:54.350
cities to adopt this model.

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:57.680
Oakland Slow Streets is trying to send

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:00.310
a message that we want Oaklanders

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:04.040
to recreate in a socially
distanced manner.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:06.040
And it's a question out there right now

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:08.280
whether some of those car-free

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:10.610
streets could be made permanent.

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:12.720
Some cities including
Seattle have announced

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:13.930
that they're going to keep theirs

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:16.720
in place indefinitely
and Oakland officials

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:18.680
have suggested that they would like to.

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:22.130
But related concerns
have come up including,

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:25.120
in Oakland, that the
implementation of these projects

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:27.130
happened too quickly and without

00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:29.540
very much input at all from neighbors.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:32.570
Including low income
communities of color who fear

00:13:32.570 --> 00:13:36.420
they could be disproportionately
policed in these spaces.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.360
Another question on the
urban planners' minds

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:40.480
right now is whether there's going to be

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:44.000
a surge in demand for
driving private vehicles

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:46.430
and therefore a surge in traffic.

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:48.750
Even though very few
cases around the world

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.650
have been linked to
riding buses or trains,

00:13:51.650 --> 00:13:54.505
there's this general
concern about overcrowding

00:13:54.505 --> 00:13:57.780
and the transmission of
coronavirus that could

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:00.410
push people away from
transit or even living

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:04.100
in dense areas where
car-free streets make sense.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:07.930
These are huge questions on
city planners' minds right now.

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:10.130
As the coronavirus transforms the world

00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:12.643
around us, the streets are no exception.

